## <span style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</span>
<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> When It's Better to Ignore Technology</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Draft a persuasive paragraph on a familiar topic. Interactive: Write daily observations about everyday situations and activities.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can draft a persuasive paragraph outlining the pros and cons of tech-free time during school hours (e.g., "While technology helps us stay informed, taking breaks can reduce eye strain and increase social interaction.").</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can write daily observations about using technology at school (e.g., "Today, I used my tablet to look up information for my science project. It helped me find everything quickly and stay organized.").</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can record observations on the benefits of technology for learning (e.g., "Using technology helps me learn faster because I can easily access resources and get immediate feedback on my work.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Tech-Free Moments Brainstorm" (5 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to think about times during the school day when technology might be a distraction or less beneficial.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students individually write down 3-4 specific situations when they believe it's better to *ignore* technology and briefly explain *why*. They *should* try to use vocabulary from previous lessons.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> (2 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes an example on the board: "I think we *should* ignore our phones during lunch because it helps us *engage* in real conversations. It's a *benefit* for our social *network*. Sometimes, the *problem* is that we don't *limit* our screen time."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models the pronunciation of "should" /ʃʊd/ and points out the stress in "benefit."</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> (3 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say we *should* ignore something, what am I doing? Am I telling you what to do, or giving advice?" (Giving advice/suggestion)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I *ignore* something, am I paying attention or not paying attention?" (Not paying attention)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short blog post excerpt: "The Digital Balance: When to Unplug at School" (7 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a short text (blog post excerpt or a student forum discussion) that discusses the benefits and drawbacks of technology in school and when it's appropriate to *limit* its *use*. The text includes examples of simple present for opinions, "should" for suggestions, and "can" for abilities. It integrates vocabulary such as *technology, education, benefit, problem, solution, device, access, instruction, appropriate*.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The text is projected or distributed, and students read it silently.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> "Understands and writes simple texts about familiar topics."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing Focus):</b> (5 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write short answers to the following questions based on the text:
            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">According to the text, what is one *benefit* of *using* *technology* for *education*?</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When *should* students consider putting their *devices* away? Write down two reasons.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What *problem* does the author identify regarding constant *access* to *apps*?</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors and provides feedback on written answers.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simple present for expressing opinions and comparing preferences, modal "should" for making suggestions, modal "can" for expressing ability, discourse markers (while, but, instead, because), target vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> (15 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Writing - Sentence Completion):</b> (5 min)
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides sentence starters on the board or a worksheet. Students complete the sentences, ensuring they use target grammar (simple present, 'should', 'can') and vocabulary.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples: "While *technology* is ______, sometimes we ______ *ignore* it *because* ______." "Students ______ *access* *resources* quickly, *but* they ______ also develop good study habits without *devices*." "*Instead* of constantly checking *apps*, we *should* ______."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher checks for correct grammar and vocabulary use in their written completions.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Writing - Sentence Transformation):</b> (5 min)
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rewrite pairs of simple sentences into more complex ones, incorporating discourse markers (*while, but, instead, because*) and modal verbs ("should," "can") to express opinions or suggestions.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples: "Technology is *useful*. It *helps* learning." → "While *technology* is *useful* and *helps* learning, we *should* also consider its *drawbacks*." "Students *prefer* *digital* *tools*. They *can* be distracting." → "*Because* students *prefer* *digital* *tools*, they *can* be distracting, *but* they *should* *learn* to *limit* their *use*."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides feedback on written sentence structure and language integration.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Guided Writing - Paragraph Outline in pairs):</b> (5 min)
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students create a simple outline for a paragraph arguing for or against tech-free time in specific school situations (e.g., during group work, in the library, during class discussions).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The outline *should* include: a topic sentence, two supporting points with brief examples or explanations, and a concluding sentence. They write keywords and phrases for each part. (e.g., Topic: "Tech-free during group work *should* be a rule." Point 1: "More *engagement*, better communication. No *device* *problem*." Point 2: "Reduces *distraction*, *focus* on *solution*." Conclusion: "*Benefits* collaboration.").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering guidance on structuring arguments and ensuring target vocabulary is considered for the outline.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual persuasive paragraph drafting (10 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Based on their outline from Activity 3, students individually draft a persuasive paragraph (6-8 sentences) on the topic: "When It's Better to Ignore Technology in School."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their paragraph *should* clearly state an opinion, provide at least two supporting reasons with examples, and use a concluding statement.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must incorporate:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two modal verbs ("should" or "can").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least three discourse markers (*while, but, instead, because*).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least five target vocabulary words (*technology, benefit, problem, solution, device, education, access, limit, appropriate*).</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages students to focus on conveying their message clearly and persuasively, using the linguistic tools practiced.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly addresses the theme "When It's Better to Ignore Technology" within the "Technology in Education" scenario, allowing students to express informed opinions in writing.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategies)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during writing and a simple rubric for the submitted paragraph.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their drafted persuasive paragraph.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric items):</b> (3 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the main opinion/argument (topic sentence).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Presence and relevance of supporting details/reasons.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target grammar: simple present (opinions), modal verbs "should" and "can."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of at least five target vocabulary words.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least three discourse markers (while, but, instead, because).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and organization of the paragraph.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> (2 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new vocabulary words you used in your paragraph about *technology*."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you think you did well in your writing today, and one thing you *should* improve next time?"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> (2 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing this persuasive paragraph, and how did you try to *solve* it?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* writing about *appropriate* *use* of *technology* help you understand its *benefits* and *problems* better?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> (2 min)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in guiding students to draft a persuasive paragraph? (e.g., specific practice activities, modeling).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next writing task, especially regarding grammar structures (simple present opinions, modals) or the use of discourse markers?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to demonstrate the learning outcomes for writing (drafting persuasive paragraphs, making daily observations, recording benefits of technology)?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In Lesson 5, students will engage in mediation and integration tasks, possibly by orally presenting a summary of their written paragraphs or exchanging written opinions with peers, then consolidating this information for their 21st-century skills project. This will involve summarizing and perhaps reformulating their written arguments for a different audience or format.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>